# Text files are checked out with LF on every platform, as the repository
# stores them. Git for Windows otherwise checks text out with CRLF
# (`core.autocrlf=true`), and a test that reads a source, a workflow or a
# template line by line then meets a trailing `\r` that nothing here writes.
# `text=auto` leaves a file git reads as binary as it is.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Source is text, even when a file contains a byte git reads as binary.
#
# Git decides binary-or-text by sniffing for a NUL, so one NUL anywhere in a
# source file — a domain separator written as a raw character rather than the
# `\0` escape, which is how this was found — makes the whole file undiffable and
# invisible to grep. Reviews then show "Bin 16144 -> 18079 bytes" in place of the
# change, and a code search for anything inside it returns nothing at all.
#
# `diff` is the attribute that settles it: a path it is set on is treated as
# text however it is spelled. It changes how such a file is shown, not how it is
# checked out, which `text=auto` above decides.
*.ts diff
*.tsx diff
*.js diff
*.jsx diff
*.mjs diff
*.cjs diff
*.css diff
*.json diff
*.md diff
